WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.312
This lesson looks at one of the worst mine disasters

00:07.312 --> 00:11.550
in modern American history. On April 5, 2010,

00:11.550 --> 00:13.700
at about three o'clock in the afternoon,

00:13.700 --> 00:16.238
a massive coal dust explosion tore through the

00:16.238 --> 00:20.250
Upper Big Branch Mine-South, in Montcoal, West Virginia.

00:20.250 --> 00:23.350
Twenty-nine miners were killed and two were injured.

00:23.350 --> 00:26.500
According to MSHA, it was the largest coal mine

00:26.500 --> 00:29.262
disaster in the United States in forty years.

00:30.409 --> 00:33.046
We're studying it carefully, and respectfully,

00:33.046 --> 00:35.146
because the lessons are about people,

00:35.146 --> 00:37.896
not just about coal. It was a coal mine,

00:37.896 --> 00:40.634
and ours are limestone, so the gases and the machines

00:40.634 --> 00:42.934
are different. But the questions it raises are

00:42.934 --> 00:45.371
the same ones you'll face at any mine.

00:45.371 --> 00:48.108
Are the exams real? Are hazards fixed?

00:48.108 --> 00:50.646
Can people speak up? Was everyone trained?

00:51.774 --> 00:54.749
Everything in this lesson comes from MSHA's public

00:54.749 --> 00:58.149
investigation report. MSHA interviewed two hundred

00:58.149 --> 01:00.861
sixty-nine people and reviewed some eighty-eight

01:00.861 --> 01:04.111
thousand pages of documents. We'll use what MSHA

01:04.111 --> 01:07.299
found and concluded, and we'll say so each time.

01:07.299 --> 01:10.324
We won't name individuals, and we won't speculate.

01:11.448 --> 01:14.310
Rescue teams went in soon after the explosion.

01:14.310 --> 01:18.010
More than twenty teams, from MSHA, the company,

01:18.010 --> 01:20.298
the state, and other mine operators,

01:20.298 --> 01:23.323
took part. Combustible gases in the mine forced

01:23.323 --> 01:26.760
the rescuers to withdraw at least three times.

01:26.760 --> 01:29.473
On April 9, the teams determined that none of the

01:29.473 --> 01:32.360
twenty-nine missing miners had survived.

01:32.360 --> 01:35.260
The last was recovered on April 13.

01:35.260 --> 01:38.048
We say this plainly, in respect for the families

01:38.048 --> 01:40.248
and for the people who went in to look for them.

01:43.570 --> 01:46.182
MSHA found that the disaster happened in three

01:46.182 --> 01:49.907
steps. First, a methane ignition. Second,

01:49.907 --> 01:52.607
a small methane explosion. And third,

01:52.607 --> 01:55.682
that small explosion set off a massive coal dust

01:55.682 --> 01:57.970
explosion that spread through the mine.

01:57.970 --> 02:01.495
MSHA's conclusion was blunt. If basic safety measures

02:01.495 --> 02:03.982
had stopped any one of those three events,

02:03.982 --> 02:06.020
there would have been no loss of life.

02:07.132 --> 02:10.307
In plain words, MSHA found that the cutting machine

02:10.307 --> 02:12.944
was running with worn bits and with water sprays

02:12.944 --> 02:16.207
missing, which gave methane an ignition source.

02:16.207 --> 02:19.432
Ventilation and roof control plans weren't followed,

02:19.432 --> 02:22.144
and that let a small amount of methane build up.

02:22.144 --> 02:25.007
And coal dust, which is fuel, had been allowed

02:25.007 --> 02:28.169
to pile up across the mine, while rock dust,

02:28.169 --> 02:30.707
which is supposed to keep that dust from burning,

02:30.707 --> 02:34.319
was grossly inadequate. MSHA said the explosion

02:34.319 --> 02:36.557
traveled about five thousand feet.

02:37.678 --> 02:40.228
Notice something. Each of these is a basic safety

02:40.228 --> 02:43.428
item. Maintain the machine. Follow the plans.

02:43.428 --> 02:46.465
Keep the dust down. Every one of them was a violation

02:46.465 --> 02:47.903
of an ordinary rule.

02:49.024 --> 02:51.999
Why does rock dust matter? Coal dust burns,

02:51.999 --> 02:55.161
and fine coal dust in the air can carry an explosion.

02:55.161 --> 02:58.086
Rock dust, which is crushed limestone spread over

02:58.086 --> 03:01.311
the surfaces, mixes with the coal dust and keeps

03:01.311 --> 03:04.311
it from burning. MSHA found that the walls,

03:04.311 --> 03:06.874
the roof, and the floor in parts of this mine were

03:06.874 --> 03:10.011
dark, which is a sign that rock dust was missing.

03:10.011 --> 03:13.061
With enough rock dust, MSHA concluded,

03:13.061 --> 03:15.549
the methane explosion would not have spread any

03:15.549 --> 03:16.449
further.

03:17.578 --> 03:20.378
In our limestone mines, the dust that matters is

03:20.378 --> 03:23.240
different, and you'll cover it in the health lessons.

03:23.240 --> 03:25.940
But the principle is universal. The hazard you

03:25.940 --> 03:28.778
leave in place is the hazard that hurts someone.

03:32.108 --> 03:35.083
MSHA also looked at the paperwork and the practices

03:35.083 --> 03:37.858
behind those conditions. Mine operators have to

03:37.858 --> 03:40.608
examine the mine for hazards before and during

03:40.608 --> 03:44.033
each shift, and weekly. MSHA found that at Upper

03:44.033 --> 03:47.258
Big Branch, examiners often didn't travel to the

03:47.258 --> 03:50.070
areas they were required to check, or went there

03:50.070 --> 03:52.808
and didn't do the required checks and measurements.

03:52.808 --> 03:55.658
In the weeks before the explosion, for example,

03:55.658 --> 03:58.145
there were no methane examinations at all on the

03:58.145 --> 04:00.933
longwall tailgate, the area where the explosion

04:00.933 --> 04:03.433
began. And when hazards were found,

04:03.433 --> 04:05.283
they were often not corrected.

04:06.398 --> 04:10.098
MSHA found that the operator kept two sets of books.

04:10.098 --> 04:12.398
One was the required examination book,

04:12.398 --> 04:16.060
which is open to review by MSHA and by miners.

04:16.060 --> 04:18.861
The other was an internal set, where hazards were

04:18.861 --> 04:21.336
sometimes written down that never made it into

04:21.336 --> 04:24.223
the required book. Miners told investigators they

04:24.223 --> 04:27.185
felt pressured not to record hazards in the required

04:27.185 --> 04:29.961
book. A record that leaves out the hazard doesn't

04:29.961 --> 04:32.798
protect anyone. It hides the hazard from the very

04:32.798 --> 04:34.348
people who could fix it.

04:35.480 --> 04:38.293
Records matter because everyone relies on them.

04:38.293 --> 04:41.255
The next shift, the inspector, and the miners all

04:41.255 --> 04:43.805
trust what's written. That's why the Mine Act makes

04:43.805 --> 04:46.330
it a crime to knowingly make a false statement

04:46.330 --> 04:49.055
or false record, including on exam records and

04:49.055 --> 04:52.918
training certificates. As a miner, you have a responsibility

04:52.918 --> 04:56.230
to tell the truth, in what you say during an inspection

04:56.230 --> 04:57.492
and in what you sign.

04:58.610 --> 05:01.222
It works both ways. A miner who is pressured to

05:01.222 --> 05:03.860
sign something that isn't true, or to leave out

05:03.860 --> 05:06.735
a hazard, should not do it, and should tell somebody

05:06.735 --> 05:09.135
who can help, which could be a supervisor higher

05:09.135 --> 05:10.785
up, or MSHA.

05:14.100 --> 05:16.900
MSHA concluded that miners at Upper Big Branch

05:16.900 --> 05:19.462
were intimidated. Miners were told that raising

05:19.462 --> 05:22.363
safety concerns would jeopardize their jobs.

05:22.363 --> 05:24.788
When safety problems delayed production,

05:24.788 --> 05:28.225
officials often responded with threats and discipline.

05:28.225 --> 05:31.663
In one instance MSHA describes, a company president

05:31.663 --> 05:34.300
was overheard threatening to get rid of an entire

05:34.300 --> 05:37.062
crew if production didn't pick up after a foreman

05:37.062 --> 05:39.550
stopped to fix a ventilation problem.

05:40.675 --> 05:43.887
The result was silence. MSHA did not receive a

05:43.887 --> 05:46.787
single safety or health complaint about underground

05:46.787 --> 05:49.399
conditions at that mine for about four years before

05:49.399 --> 05:52.425
the explosion, even though MSHA has a toll-free

05:52.425 --> 05:55.024
hotline that miners can use without giving their

05:55.024 --> 05:58.175
name. Miners testified they were reluctant to use

05:58.175 --> 06:00.487
even the company's own safety line,

06:00.487 --> 06:02.487
for fear of retaliation.

06:03.600 --> 06:06.712
MSHA also found that the operator routinely gave

06:06.712 --> 06:09.675
miners advance notice when inspectors arrived,

06:09.675 --> 06:11.950
so that hazards could be fixed or hidden before

06:11.950 --> 06:14.725
the inspector got there. Under the Mine Act,

06:14.725 --> 06:17.562
advance notice of an inspection is illegal.

06:18.690 --> 06:21.715
And MSHA found a training failure. The operator

06:21.715 --> 06:24.865
did not provide required experienced-miner training,

06:24.865 --> 06:27.827
especially on hazard recognition. It did not give

06:27.827 --> 06:30.402
task training to people starting new jobs,

06:30.402 --> 06:33.277
and it did not give the required annual refresher.

06:33.277 --> 06:36.177
MSHA wrote that this left miners unequipped to

06:36.177 --> 06:38.665
identify and correct hazards.

06:41.980 --> 06:45.255
What changed afterward? MSHA issued an emergency

06:45.255 --> 06:50.580
rock dust standard in 2010, and a final rule in 2011.

06:50.580 --> 06:54.492
In 2012, a new rule required operators of underground

06:54.492 --> 06:57.542
coal mines to examine work areas for violations

06:57.542 --> 06:59.892
of certain standards and record them.

06:59.892 --> 07:04.055
In 2013, MSHA strengthened its pattern of violations

07:04.055 --> 07:06.805
program, which targets mines that keep breaking

07:06.805 --> 07:10.567
the rules. MSHA also began impact inspections at

07:10.567 --> 07:12.942
mines with a history of noncompliance,

07:12.942 --> 07:15.930
and reviewed its own actions before the explosion.

07:17.137 --> 07:19.662
For metal and nonmetal mines like ours,

07:19.662 --> 07:22.587
one of the key rules is the workplace examination

07:22.587 --> 07:25.274
rule, which has the same principle behind it.

07:26.404 --> 07:29.042
The rule says a competent person examines each

07:29.042 --> 07:31.592
working place at least once each shift,

07:31.592 --> 07:34.567
before miners begin work there. If the examiner

07:34.567 --> 07:37.217
finds something, miners in the affected area are

07:37.217 --> 07:40.004
told promptly, and the operator acts to correct

07:40.004 --> 07:43.179
it. A record is made before the end of the shift,

07:43.179 --> 07:45.667
with the examiner's name, the date,

07:45.667 --> 07:48.654
every area examined, and a description of each

07:48.654 --> 07:51.629
condition found. When a condition is corrected,

07:51.629 --> 07:54.042
the record gets the date. And the records must

07:54.042 --> 07:57.254
be kept at least a year, and shown to MSHA.

07:58.390 --> 08:00.828
That one rule is basically the opposite of two

08:00.828 --> 08:02.128
sets of books.

08:05.456 --> 08:08.319
So what's your part? First, report hazards,

08:08.319 --> 08:11.181
every time, and don't assume somebody else did.

08:11.181 --> 08:13.381
At Allen you can tell your supervisor,

08:13.381 --> 08:16.106
note equipment problems on your pre-trip inspection,

08:16.106 --> 08:19.756
or log an observation in the HCSS app. Second,

08:19.756 --> 08:22.981
be truthful in anything you sign or record.

08:22.981 --> 08:25.456
Third, ask for training when you're asked to do

08:25.456 --> 08:28.381
something new. And fourth, remember that you have

08:28.381 --> 08:30.519
protection, and you'll learn how to use it in the

08:30.519 --> 08:31.506
next lesson.

08:32.642 --> 08:34.417
What if you ever see something like this at your

08:34.417 --> 08:37.055
own mine? If you're told to leave out a hazard,

08:37.055 --> 08:39.805
to skip an exam, or to stay quiet about something

08:39.805 --> 08:42.842
unsafe, don't go along with it. Report it up the

08:42.842 --> 08:46.117
line, to your supervisor, your site supervisor,

08:46.117 --> 08:49.242
or Hunter Kenney, who is over all of our sites.

08:49.242 --> 08:52.055
And you always have another route. MSHA's hazard

08:52.055 --> 08:55.417
hotline is one eight hundred, seven four six,

08:55.417 --> 08:58.942
fifteen fifty-three, open all day, every day,

08:58.942 --> 09:00.692
and you don't have to give your name.

09:01.820 --> 09:04.208
The next lesson covers exactly how to use these

09:04.208 --> 09:05.120
rights.

09:06.254 --> 09:09.717
To sum up. Upper Big Branch was a coal dust explosion

09:09.717 --> 09:13.454
that began as a methane ignition, and MSHA found

09:13.454 --> 09:16.979
it was preventable. It happened amid skipped examinations,

09:16.979 --> 09:19.429
hidden hazards, fear of speaking up,

09:19.429 --> 09:22.567
advance notice of inspections, and missing training.

09:22.567 --> 09:24.904
The rules that followed, and the rules you work

09:24.904 --> 09:27.692
under now, exist so that a person at the bottom

09:27.692 --> 09:29.879
of the line can say something is wrong,

09:29.879 --> 09:30.854
and be heard.

09:31.988 --> 09:34.963
Your site's plans always override this video.
